          "en": "SHA-256 is an excellent hash function... for checking the integrity of a file. It is designed\nto be **fast**, and that is exactly the problem: a single graphics card tests **billions** of\ncandidate passwords per second. The attacker does not \"decrypt\" anything, they try\ndictionaries of common passwords and compare.\n\nTwo more defects appear when hashing carelessly:\n\n- **No salt**: two users with the same password get the same hash, and precomputed tables\n  (*rainbow tables*) give the answer instantly.\n- **No tunable cost**: there is no way to slow the computation down as hardware improves.\n\nA **password** hashing function fixes these three points:\n\n1. A random **salt**, unique per user, stored next to the hash. It is not secret: it makes\n   every hash different and precomputed tables useless.\n2. A **cost factor**: the computation deliberately takes 100 to 500 ms. Invisible to a user\n   who logs in, ruinous for an attacker who must try billions of candidates.\n3. For Argon2 and scrypt, a **memory** cost: each attempt consumes tens of MiB, which\n   neutralises the advantage of graphics cards.\n"
